GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60G(15) the bar on granting the application for PACC permission applies` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if an application for PACC permission is made by a PACP, and prior to the making of that application, the applicant is found by the Court of Appeal ... to have abused the process of the court" -- the "if" is not met, so the bar does not apply
        IF NOT `the applicant was found by the Court of Appeal, before making the application, to have abused the process of the court as paragraph (a) or (b) describes` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless -- (c) the PACP adduces material ... not adduced in court before the date of the most recent such finding; and (d) the material could not, even with reasonable diligence, have been adduced" -- the unless is met, so the bar is lifted
        IF `the PACP adduces material that was not adduced in court before the date of the most recent finding of abuse of process` AND `the material could not, even with reasonable diligence, have been adduced in court before that date` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The prohibition is derived from the answer above (ruling N2): the text names its bearer, "the Court of Appeal".
DECLARE `s 60G(15) party` IS ONE OF `s 60G(15) the Court of Appeal`
DECLARE `s 60G(15) act` IS ONE OF `s 60G(15) grants the application for PACC permission although the applicant has been found to have abused the process of the court and has not adduced new material`

-- src:3720 | (15) Despite any provision in this section, if an application for
-- src:3721 | PACC permission is made by a PACP, and prior to the making of that
-- src:3722 | application, the applicant is found by the Court of Appeal —
-- src:3724 | (a) to have abused the process of the court in relation to a
-- src:3725 | relevant application that was filed on or after the date of
-- src:3726 | commencement of the Post‑appeal Applications in Capital
-- src:3727 | Cases Act 2022; or
-- src:3729 | (b) to have abused the process of the court in order to delay or
-- src:3730 | frustrate the carrying out of the sentence of death in
-- src:3731 | relation to an application (other than a relevant application)
-- src:3732 | or action that was filed on or after the date mentioned in
-- src:3733 | paragraph (a),
-- src:3735 | the Court of Appeal must not grant the application for PACC
-- src:3736 | permission unless —
-- src:3738 | (c) the PACP adduces material (being evidence or legal
-- src:3739 | arguments) that was not adduced in court before the date
-- src:3740 | of the most recent of any such finding of abuse of process
-- src:3741 | by the Court of Appeal; and
-- src:3743 | (d) the material could not, even with reasonable diligence,
-- src:3744 | have been adduced in court before the date mentioned in
-- src:3745 | paragraph (c).
@ref s 60G(15) (src:3720-3745)
`s 60G(15) the prohibition on the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    PARTY `s 60G(15) the Court of Appeal`
    SHANT `s 60G(15) grants the application for PACC permission although the applicant has been found to have abused the process of the court and has not adduced new material`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `s 60G(15) the Court of Appeal` BECAUSE "the Court of Appeal granted an application for PACC permission although the applicant had been found to have abused the process of the court and had not adduced new material"

#TRACE `s 60G(15) the prohibition on the Court of Appeal` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 60G(15) the Court of Appeal` DOES `s 60G(15) grants the application for PACC permission although the applicant has been found to have abused the process of the court and has not adduced new material` AT 1

§§ `s 60H -- Hearing of PACC application`

-- src:3751 | 60H.—(1) Subject to section 60I, where the Court of Appeal grants
-- src:3752 | the application for PACC permission, the PACC application must be
-- src:3753 | made to the Court of Appeal, and fixed for hearing, within such
-- src:3754 | period as is prescribed by the Rules of Court.
@ref s 60H(1) (src:3751-3754)
GIVEN `section 60I applies to the PACP` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court of Appeal grants the application for PACC permission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period the Rules of Court prescribe for making and fixing the PACC application, in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the number of days taken to make it and fix it for hearing` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(1) the PACC application is made to the Court of Appeal and fixed for hearing within the prescribed period, as the section requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Subject to section 60I" -- section 60I governs where it applies
        IF `section 60I applies to the PACP` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the Court of Appeal grants the application for PACC permission" -- the "where" is not met
        IF NOT `the Court of Appeal grants the application for PACC permission` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the period the Rules of Court prescribe for making and fixing the PACC application, in days`
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "within such period as is prescribed by the Rules of Court" -- the Rules of Court are not among the sources
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST period THEN
                IF `the number of days taken to make it and fix it for hearing` AT MOST period
                THEN JUST TRUE
                -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "must be made to the Court of Appeal, and fixed for hearing, within such period as is prescribed" -- a bare "must" not done within a period the Rules of Court prescribe (not fixed in the text, so the period ruling does not apply): the text states the duty and does not say what follows
                ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3756 | (2) The applicant in a PACC application must file such documents
-- src:3757 | in support of that application, within such period, as are prescribed in
-- src:3758 | the Rules of Court.
@ref s 60H(2) (src:3756-3758)
GIVEN `the period the Rules of Court prescribe for the applicant's documents, in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the number of days taken to file them` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(2) the applicant files the documents in support within the prescribed period, as the section requires` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period the Rules of Court prescribe for the applicant's documents, in days`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "within such period, as are prescribed in the Rules of Court" -- the Rules of Court are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST period THEN
        IF `the number of days taken to file them` AT MOST period
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "must file such documents ... within such period" -- a bare "must" not done within a period the Rules of Court prescribe (not fixed in the text, so the period ruling does not apply): the text states the duty and does not say what follows
        ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3765 | (3) The respondent in a PACC application must file such documents
-- src:3766 | in relation to that application, within such period, as are prescribed in
-- src:3767 | the Rules of Court.
@ref s 60H(3) (src:3765-3767)
GIVEN `the period the Rules of Court prescribe for the respondent's documents, in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the number of days taken to file them` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(3) the respondent files the documents in relation to the application within the prescribed period, as the section requires` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period the Rules of Court prescribe for the respondent's documents, in days`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "within such period, as are prescribed in the Rules of Court" -- the Rules of Court are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST period THEN
        IF `the number of days taken to file them` AT MOST period
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "must file such documents ... within such period" -- a bare "must" not done within a period the Rules of Court prescribe (not fixed in the text, so the period ruling does not apply): the text states the duty and does not say what follows
        ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3769 | (4) The Registrar or any Supreme Court Judge may extend or
-- src:3770 | abridge any period mentioned in subsection (1), (2) or (3).
@ref s 60H(4) (src:3769-3770)
GIVEN `the person is the Registrar or a Supreme Court Judge` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period is one mentioned in subsection (1), (2) or (3)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(4) the person may extend or abridge the period` MEANS
    IF `the person is the Registrar or a Supreme Court Judge` AND `the period is one mentioned in subsection (1), (2) or (3)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is the Registrar's or a Supreme Court Judge's, over a period mentioned in subsection (1), (2) or (3)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3772 | (5) The Court of Appeal which hears a PACC application may
-- src:3773 | exercise any power and make any order that could have been
-- src:3774 | exercised and made by the Appellate Division or the General
-- src:3775 | Division (whether or not exercising its original jurisdiction).
@ref s 60H(5) (src:3772-3775)
GIVEN `it is the Court of Appeal that hears a PACC application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the power or order is one that the Appellate Division or the General Division could have exercised or made, whether or not exercising its original jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(5) the Court of Appeal may exercise the power and make the order` MEANS
    IF `it is the Court of Appeal that hears a PACC application` AND `the power or order is one that the Appellate Division or the General Division could have exercised or made, whether or not exercising its original jurisdiction`
    -- the Singapore International Commercial Court is not named here: whether "the General Division" includes it is not settled (K1), and the input asks for the General Division's or the Appellate Division's power
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the grant is of any power exercised or order made "by the Appellate Division or the General Division"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3777 | (6) A PACC application may, without being set down for hearing,
-- src:3778 | be summarily dealt with by a written order of the Court of Appeal.
@ref s 60H(6) (src:3777-3778)
GIVEN `it is a PACC application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(6) the PACC application may be summarily dealt with by a written order without being set down for hearing` MEANS
    IF `it is a PACC application`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks of "A PACC application"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3783 | (a) must consider the applicant’s written submissions, if any;
-- src:3784 | and
@ref s 60H(7)(a) (src:3783-3784)
GIVEN `it considered the applicant's written submissions, if any` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(7)(a) the Court of Appeal has considered the applicant's written submissions before summarily refusing, as the section requires` MEANS
    IF `it considered the applicant's written submissions, if any`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "Before summarily refusing a PACC application under subsection (6), the Court of Appeal -- (a) must consider the applicant's written submissions, if any" -- a bare "must" not done: the text states the duty and does not say what follows
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3786 | (b) may, but is not required to, consider the respondent’s
-- src:3787 | written submissions, if any.
@ref s 60H(7)(b) (src:3786-3787)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal is summarily refusing the PACC application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(7)(b) the Court of Appeal is required to consider the respondent's written submissions before summarily refusing` MEANS
    IF `the Court of Appeal is summarily refusing the PACC application`
    THEN
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) may, but is not required to, consider the respondent's written submissions, if any"
        JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph speaks of the Court before summarily refusing a PACC application
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3789 | (8) Except where subsection (7) applies, before summarily deciding
-- src:3790 | a PACC application on its merits, the Court of Appeal —
-- src:3792 | (a) must consider the applicant’s written submissions, if any;
-- src:3793 | and
-- src:3795 | (b) must consider the respondent’s written submissions, if any.
@ref s 60H(8) (src:3789-3795)
GIVEN `subsection (7) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it considered the applicant's written submissions, if any` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it considered the respondent's written submissions, if any` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(8) the Court of Appeal has considered both sets of written submissions before summarily deciding the PACC application on its merits, as the section requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Except where subsection (7) applies"
        IF `subsection (7) applies` THEN NOTHING
        IF `it considered the applicant's written submissions, if any` AND `it considered the respondent's written submissions, if any` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "before summarily deciding a PACC application on its merits, the Court of Appeal -- (a) must consider the applicant's written submissions, if any; and (b) must consider the respondent's written submissions, if any" -- a bare "must" not done: the text states the duty and does not say what follows
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:3797 | (9) The Court of Appeal may —
-- src:3799 | (a) before deciding a PACC application;
-- src:3801 | (b) when granting a PACC application; or
-- src:3803 | (c) when summarily granting a PACC application,
-- src:3805 | do one or both of the following:
-- src:3807 | (d) make any incidental directions or give any interim orders
-- src:3808 | (including a stay of execution of the death sentence) as the
-- src:3809 | Court considers necessary;
-- src:3811 | (e) take additional evidence.
@ref s 60H(9) (src:3797-3811)
GIVEN stage IS A `s 60G(11) stage`
      measure IS A `s 60G(11) measure`
      `the Court considers it necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(9) the Court of Appeal may take the measure at the stage of the PACC application` MEANS
    CONSIDER stage
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) before deciding ...; (b) when granting ...; or (c) when summarily granting ..." -- "when" counts as "where" (ruling of 2026-11-05), so a stage outside the three is the text's no (fork F-when, NOTES.md)
    WHEN `s 60G(11) at another stage` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER measure
        WHEN `s 60G(11)(d) making an incidental direction or giving an interim order, including a stay of execution of the death sentence` THEN
            IF `the Court considers it necessary`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "as the Court considers necessary"
            ELSE NOTHING
        WHEN `s 60G(11)(e) taking additional evidence` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is to do one or both of the two things in paragraphs (d) and (e)
        WHEN `s 60G(11) another measure` THEN NOTHING

-- src:3818 | (10) The Court of Appeal may, if it thinks additional evidence needs
-- src:3819 | to be taken under subsection (9), either take such evidence itself or
-- src:3820 | have such evidence taken by one of the Judges of the Court of Appeal.
@ref s 60H(10) (src:3818-3820)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal thinks additional evidence needs to be taken under subsection (9)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60H(10) the Court of Appeal may either take the evidence itself or have it taken by one of the Judges of the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    IF `the Court of Appeal thinks additional evidence needs to be taken under subsection (9)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may, if it thinks additional evidence needs to be taken under subsection (9)" -- the "if" is not met
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 60I -- Procedure for making PACC application, etc., when there is pending PACC application, etc.`

-- src:3827 | 60I.—(1) Where any application for PACC permission, or any
-- src:3828 | PACC application, made by a PACP (each called in this section the
-- src:3829 | specified application) is pending determination, the PACP cannot
-- src:3830 | make a subsequent application for PACC permission or a subsequent
-- src:3831 | PACC application (each called in this section the subsequent
-- src:3832 | application) unless the PACP has the permission of the Court of
-- src:3833 | Appeal dealing with the specified application to do so.
@ref s 60I(1) (src:3827-3833)
GIVEN `a specified application (an application for PACC permission or a PACC application made by the PACP) is pending determination` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the PACP has the permission of the Court of Appeal dealing with the specified application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60I(1) the PACP can make a subsequent application for PACC permission or a subsequent PACC application` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "Where any application for PACC permission, or any PACC application, made by a PACP ... is pending determination" -- the "where" is not met, so the prohibition is not triggered and the text is silent on whether the PACP may make a subsequent application (that is for the other provisions of this Division); the bar-phrased function below asks the prohibition's own question and answers JUST FALSE here (ruling of 2026-11-05, part (b))
        IF NOT `a specified application (an application for PACC permission or a PACC application made by the PACP) is pending determination` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "unless" -- the unless is met, which lifts the bar without itself granting the application
        IF `the PACP has the permission of the Court of Appeal dealing with the specified application` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the PACP cannot make a subsequent application ... unless the PACP has the permission of the Court of Appeal dealing with the specified application to do so"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:3827 | 60I.—(1) Where any application for PACC permission, or any
-- src:3828 | PACC application, made by a PACP (each called in this section the
-- src:3829 | specified application) is pending determination, the PACP cannot
-- src:3830 | make a subsequent application for PACC permission or a subsequent
-- src:3831 | PACC application (each called in this section the subsequent
-- src:3832 | application) unless the PACP has the permission of the Court of
-- src:3833 | Appeal dealing with the specified application to do so.
@ref s 60I(1) (src:3827-3833)
GIVEN `a specified application (an application for PACC permission or a PACC application made by the PACP) is pending determination` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the PACP has the permission of the Court of Appeal dealing with the specified application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60I(1) the bar on a subsequent application applies to the PACP` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where any application ... is pending determination" -- the "where" is not met, so the bar does not apply
        IF NOT `a specified application (an application for PACC permission or a PACC application made by the PACP) is pending determination` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the PACP has the permission of the Court of Appeal dealing with the specified application to do so" -- the unless is met, so the bar is lifted
        IF `the PACP has the permission of the Court of Appeal dealing with the specified application` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The prohibition is derived from the answer above (ruling N2): the text names its bearer, "the PACP".
DECLARE `s 60I(1) party` IS ONE OF `s 60I(1) the PACP`
DECLARE `s 60I(1) act` IS ONE OF `s 60I(1) makes a subsequent application for PACC permission or a subsequent PACC application while a specified application is pending and without the permission of the Court of Appeal`

-- src:3827 | 60I.—(1) Where any application for PACC permission, or any
-- src:3828 | PACC application, made by a PACP (each called in this section the
-- src:3829 | specified application) is pending determination, the PACP cannot
-- src:3830 | make a subsequent application for PACC permission or a subsequent
-- src:3831 | PACC application (each called in this section the subsequent
